1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:03,329
Farmers usually water a whole field the same way.

2
00:00:03,329 --> 00:00:07,219
The dry patches only show once the plants are already suffering.

3
00:00:08,317 --> 00:00:11,788
A soil probe tells you exactly how wet the ground is,

4
00:00:11,788 --> 00:00:13,164
but only at one spot,

5
00:00:13,164 --> 00:00:16,898
and every push into the soil takes ten to thirty seconds.

6
00:00:16,898 --> 00:00:19,453
Nobody can probe a whole field by hand.

7
00:00:20,550 --> 00:00:22,768
Today's options each miss something.

8
00:00:22,768 --> 00:00:25,233
Fixed probes measure one point per zone.

9
00:00:25,233 --> 00:00:28,436
Handheld meters need a person to walk to every spot.

10
00:00:28,436 --> 00:00:31,332
Satellites see pixels of twenty metres or more.

11
00:00:31,332 --> 00:00:35,707
Cosmic-ray sensors average over a radius of more than a hundred metres.

12
00:00:35,707 --> 00:00:38,541
Tractors cannot drive through a standing crop,

13
00:00:38,541 --> 00:00:41,190
coring robots measure nutrients, not water,

14
00:00:41,190 --> 00:00:45,811
and scouting robots under the canopy estimate water stress only indirectly,

15
00:00:45,811 --> 00:00:46,427
if at all.

16
00:00:47,517 --> 00:00:51,567
Research robots that push a probe into the soil do exist,

17
00:00:51,567 --> 00:00:55,049
but they plan in open space, visit preset points,

18
00:00:55,049 --> 00:00:56,968
or skip mapping altogether.

19
00:00:56,968 --> 00:01:00,663
And hardware-first farm robots keep failing on cost:

20
00:01:00,663 --> 00:01:04,571
Mineral and Small Robot Company both shut down in 2024.

21
00:01:04,571 --> 00:01:07,485
So we prove the idea in simulation first.

22
00:01:08,550 --> 00:01:11,799
agrisim is a simulator of a planted tomato field.

23
00:01:11,799 --> 00:01:15,977
A small rover drives only in the lanes between the rows, stops,

24
00:01:15,977 --> 00:01:17,038
probes the soil,

25
00:01:17,038 --> 00:01:21,680
and turns a few dozen readings into a moisture map of the whole field,

26
00:01:21,680 --> 00:01:23,338
with its own uncertainty.

27
00:01:23,338 --> 00:01:26,389
agribench scores it against published methods,

28
00:01:26,389 --> 00:01:28,113
on fields nobody has seen.

29
00:01:29,183 --> 00:01:32,196
The rover only drives on the lane centre lines,

30
00:01:32,196 --> 00:01:35,144
and reverses instead of turning inside a lane.

31
00:01:35,144 --> 00:01:38,284
Every pose is checked against every nearby plant.

32
00:01:38,284 --> 00:01:42,450
In fifteen thousand two hundred and thirty two benchmark surveys,

33
00:01:42,450 --> 00:01:43,924
it touched zero plants.

34
00:01:45,017 --> 00:01:47,350
Each reading becomes a coloured flag,

35
00:01:47,350 --> 00:01:50,188
and the map fills in between them by kriging.

36
00:01:50,188 --> 00:01:51,702
Here is the robot's map,

37
00:01:51,702 --> 00:01:53,405
and then the real moisture,

38
00:01:53,405 --> 00:01:55,108
which the robot never sees.

39
00:01:56,217 --> 00:01:58,024
Our strategy, planned survey,

40
00:01:58,024 --> 00:02:02,075
first covers the field on a route that fits the mission deadline.

41
00:02:02,075 --> 00:02:08,431
Then it spends its last probes where a reading is most likely to flip a spot between too dry and fine.

42
00:02:08,431 --> 00:02:11,236
That is the decision a farmer actually makes.

43
00:02:12,350 --> 00:02:13,340
On fresh fields,

44
00:02:13,340 --> 00:02:17,301
planned survey scores seventy five point eight out of a hundred.

45
00:02:17,301 --> 00:02:22,871
That is ahead of our re-implementations of spatial coverage sampling at sixty point seven,

46
00:02:22,871 --> 00:02:25,533
Moisture Mapper at forty eight point three,

47
00:02:25,533 --> 00:02:28,627
and greedy mutual information at thirty point six.

48
00:02:29,717 --> 00:02:33,158
In our comparison with the closest published systems,

49
00:02:33,158 --> 00:02:35,430
only agrisim counts plant contacts,

50
00:02:35,430 --> 00:02:38,157
and lets the soil change during a mission,

51
00:02:38,157 --> 00:02:39,521
with rain and drying.

52
00:02:39,521 --> 00:02:43,157
What we do not have yet is a real robot in a real field.

53
00:02:44,250 --> 00:02:46,503
The rover design follows the physics.

54
00:02:46,503 --> 00:02:49,426
The probe sits in an open bay between the axles,

55
00:02:49,426 --> 00:02:53,567
the only place where the whole weight of the rover resists the push.

56
00:02:53,567 --> 00:02:56,794
A rocker chassis keeps all four wheels on the ground,

57
00:02:56,794 --> 00:02:59,717
and the probe cleans itself after every reading.

58
00:03:00,817 --> 00:03:06,001
Every soil number is still a placeholder until we calibrate with real field data,

59
00:03:06,001 --> 00:03:09,521
and our map error still ties the best coverage designs.

60
00:03:09,521 --> 00:03:10,801
Next come real data,

61
00:03:10,801 --> 00:03:12,529
a per-zone irrigation plan,

62
00:03:12,529 --> 00:03:14,001
and building the rover.

63
00:03:15,083 --> 00:03:16,542
agrisim. Open source,

64
00:03:16,542 --> 00:03:18,347
and it runs on any laptop.
